  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nIn the wake of a series of upper level systems earlier this week,\na weak ridge embedded in northwesterly flow will bring a period \nof warmer but breezy weather to North Central Montana Wednesday \nand Thursday. This period of warmer weather will come to a quick \nend on Friday as a decaying upper level trough moves through \nSouthern British Columbia and Alberta into Montana, which will \nbring cooler temperatures, along with some showers of rain and \nmountain snowfall as it pushes through. The cooler temperatures \nwill linger behind this system, which will set up the next, \nstronger system to produce widespread precipitation with snow \nexpected above 7000 feet, which would include some of the higher \nmountain passes. A brief period of warmer and drier weather is \nexpected late Sunday and Monday before the unsettled pattern \nresumes by the middle of next week.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWinds today and Thursday: Westerly, cross barrier flow will bring\nsome gusty winds to North Central and Southwestern Montana \nWednesday and Thursday. While winds are not expected to be strong \nenough to reach high wind criteria (58 mph for most, 75 mph along \nthe Rocky Mountain Front), gusts to 35-50 mph will be possible \nacross the area, especially on Thursday when winds will be \nstrongest. \n\nMountain Snowfall this weekend: A strong upper level low will move\ninto the Treasure State over the weekend, bringing well below\naverage temperatures along with copious amounts of moisture. The\ncooler temperatures will bring snow levels down to 7000 feet above\nsea level, which will result in widespread mountain snowfall to\nareas above 7000 feet. Current forecasts keep snowfall amounts\nunder 6 inches at the moment, but some of the highest terrain\nstill have around a 10 percent chance of snowfall amounts over a\nfoot over the weekend. Ludwig",
